    "# Gravitational Lens Modeling of COSJ100013+023424\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook fits a real strong gravitational lens system using PyAutoLens\n",
    "\n",
    "## Quick physics primer: what is strong gravitational lensing?\n",
    "\n",
    "General relativity tells us that mass curves spacetime, and light follows that\n",
    "curvature. A sufficiently massive foreground object (here, a galaxy) acts like\n",
    "an imperfect lens: light from a more distant background galaxy, passing close\n",
    "to the foreground galaxy, gets bent. If the alignment between observer,\n",
    "lens, and source is close enough, the background galaxy's light can be\n",
    "smeared into arcs, or even a complete ring (an \"Einstein ring\") around the\n",
    "lens galaxy.\n",
    "\n",
    "Two numbers define the basic geometry of any such system:\n",
    "- **Einstein radius**: the characteristic angular scale (in arcseconds) of the\n",
    "  lensing effect -- roughly, the radius at which the arcs/ring appear. It\n",
    "  depends on the lens mass and the relative distances between us, the lens,\n",
    "  and the source.\n",
    "- **Redshift** (z) of the lens and source: because the universe is expanding,\n",
    "  more distant objects have their light stretched to longer (redder)\n",
    "  wavelengths. Redshift is our proxy for distance, and it's essential for\n",
    "  lensing calculations because the *relative* distances between observer,\n",
    "  lens, and source determine the strength of the lensing effect.\n",
    "\n",
    "**This specific system:**\n",
    "- RA = 150.100467 deg, Dec = 1.893029 deg (J2000) -- its position on the sky\n",
    "- Lens redshift z = 2.02 (photometric estimate)\n",
    "- Einstein radius = 0. arcsec (previously measured, published value)\n",
    "- Source redshift: **not confirmed from public tables** -- we use a\n",
    "  placeholder of z=2.0 below, clearly flagged where it appears. This doesn't\n",
    "  prevent us from fitting the lens *mass* model, but it would matter for any\n",
    "  physical (rather than purely angular/image-based) quantities computed from\n",
    "  the source.\n",
    "\n",
    "Known `autolens` install bugs are pre-patched throughout this notebook -- look\n",
    "for `# BUG WORKAROUND` comments explaining each one as it comes up.\n"
   ]
  },
